VERDICT: PASS -- every expectation the case states is met.
===========================================================================

  "Matching traffic should be permitted/denied depending on ACL"
      permit host 2001:db8:1::1  -> 10/10 forwarded   (base 10/10)
      deny  2001:db8:2::/64      ->  0/10 forwarded   (base 10/10)
      ACL counters confirm both rules took 10 hits each -- the drop is the
      ACL acting, not a dead path.

  "non IPv6 traffic not affected. ARPs not affected"
      IPv4 ICMP  -> 10/10 forwarded with the IPv6 ACL applied
      ARP        -> 10/10 forwarded with the IPv6 ACL applied

  "First match algorithm applies"                         PROVEN, follow-up B
      permit host 2001:db8:2::5   (seq 4)
      deny   2001:db8:2::/64      (seq 8)
      2001:db8:2::5 -> 10/10 forwarded  (the EARLIER permit wins)
      2001:db8:2::6 -> 0/10            (same /64, only the deny matches)
      Counters show the hits landing on the permit rule.

BEHAVIOUR WORTH KNOWING -- NO IMPLICIT DENY ON THIS PATH
  Traffic matching NO rule is FORWARDED, not dropped:
      2001:db8:9::1 with only permit+deny rules present -> 10/10 forwarded
  Adding an explicit trailing rule changes it:
      ... + "deny ipv6 any any"  ->  2001:db8:9::1  0/10 forwarded
  So a hardware IPv6 ACL applied with `ipv6 traffic-filter` filters only what
  it explicitly matches; there is no implicit deny-all at the end. If you want
  default-deny you must write `deny ipv6 any any` yourself.
  RECORDED AS AN OBSERVATION, NOT A FAILURE: this case does not ask for an
  implicit deny -- it asks that matching traffic be permitted/denied and that
  non-IPv6 traffic be unaffected, which it is. My first run graded an
  "implicit deny" probe FAIL against my own assumption rather than the case
  text; that grading was wrong and is corrected here. Whether default-deny is
  expected on the IE520 is the question to raise, and it is what AWPTCM-T38413
  step 3 would have covered had a standard list been port-applicable.

NOTE ON SCOPE NOT COVERED
  The case also says "Apply ACL via service policy (QoS) and repeat tests".
  That half was not run here -- it belongs with the QoS group and needs a
  class-map/policy-map built around the same ACL. Recorded so it is not
  mistaken for a clean sweep.

CONFIG LEFT BEHIND: none. Both lists removed, traffic-filter removed from
port2.0.2, `show ipv6 access-list` empty at exit.
